ntrols  to make sure that we can maintain these  requirements  the system that we've designed for these  principles  looks slightly different than a normal  hsm in the cloud model  in the traditional model you would have  crypto op access represented by the blue  arrows  and admin access represented by the red  arrows  to your own partition on the hsm the  cloud provider would have admin access  to the device as a whole  which they would use for operations like  setting the hsm up initially for you  in contrast in cloud hsm you only get  crypto op  access that access is to  shared partitions on the hsm and it's  mediated by the cloud hsm service  google retains admin access to both the  partitions  and the hsm device the cloud hsm service  is responsible for load balancing and  controlling  access to the hsm it can see plain texts  but can't see key material since these  are fips devices  raw key material must remain on the hsm  instead the service can see wrapped key  material  which it uses extensively in  the system to make it scalable and  highly available  each region has a wrapping key that's  replicated to a partition  on all of the hsms in the region when  you create a key  the service wraps your key with the  region's wrapping key  and collects the attestation it puts the  wrapped key  in an external database when you want to  do a crypto operation  the service will retrieve the wrap key  from the database  and send it to an hsm along with the  inputs to the crypto operation  for example with the plain text in an  encryption  the hsm will unwrap the key and do the  operation  this scheme has a few benefits first  as soon as your key is created any hsm  in the region can use it immediately  there's no synchronization mechanism  that needs to happen  online second  the durability of your keys isn't tied  to  the hsms your keys are all backed up in  an external database  and third regardless of the number of  operations that you have or the number  of keys that you have  all customers experience the  availability of being backed  by a full region's worth of hsms in  order to scale up in this model  we'll install a new hsm in the data  center  for the region and clone the region's  wrapping key to that new hsm  additionally if the region is supposed  to support a multi-region  we'll clone the multi-region wrapping  key to a separate partition  for example here we have three cloud  regions  the first two of which also support the  same multi-region  the hsms in the first two cloud regions  have  a second partition that is cloned with  the same multi-regional wrapping key  the hsms and cloud region 3 don't have  the second partition  because the region doesn't support the  multi-region  when you create a key in a multi-region  the process looks very similar to that  as a region cloud  the cloud sim service creates the key in  the partition and wraps it  this means that as soon as you create a  key in a multi-region  it can be used immediately anywhere in  that multi-region  we bootstrap off the rest of google's  synchronized databases  also by using separate wrapping keys  for different regions and different  multi-regions  the service ensures that keys can't be  used  outside their intended region for  example  a key wrapped with the region 1 wrapping  key  can't be used in cloud region 2 because  none of the hsms in region 2 can load  that key  likewise a key wrapped with the  multi-regional wrapping key  can't be used in cloud region 3 for the  same reason  this ensures that once we've set the  system up  it automatically enforces  regionalization constraints  setting the system up that is cloning  the wrapping keys across different hsms  is an administrative operation and we  have a different set of controls  to make sure that happens safely first  only a small number of googlers  have permission to perform admin  operations  the operations require mvn authorization  on the hsms so the hsms require multiple  googlers to  authorize each operation and googlers  need to provide two-factor  authentication in order to do so  all the operations happen in a secure  and highly restricted access  facility we have extensive audit logs  both for access to the facility and  all the operations that happen inside  additionally as also mentioned that  these are fips devices  they don't allow specific operations  regardless of who is doing them even hsm  administrator  for example you can't export plain text  key material  of either customer keys or wrapping keys  and finally many of our systems are  built in such a way to  not allow hsm administrators to perform  crypto operations  even with administrator credentials we  have all these mechanisms in place  to keep you safe from googlers other  customers  on the other hand only have crypto  operation access  so to keep you safe from them we have a  different set of mechanisms  first when a customer does an operation  we check whether they have permission to  do the operation  in cloud iam by using cloud iam  you can use the same set of permissions  and policies that you use to manage the  rest of your cloud resources  to also manage your hsm keys second  we check that the customer has  sufficient quota to do the operation  in order to make sure that we have  enough resources  to serve requests for your workloads  when you need them  we need to make sure that other  customers aren't unexpectedly  overloading the service  and third we have basic request  validation before the request reaches  the hsm  for example we check that different  arguments are within their expected  range in size and also the key types are  correct  this coupled with the fact that there is  no customer admin access  significantly reduces the attack surface  available  creating a safer product in summary  we've covered  quite a few details about about how the  cloud hsm  service works we've seen how we use  key wrapping and partitions to make a  highly available  and scalable service that takes away the  need  for you to manage your own hsm devices  we've seen how this system keeps you  safe  from both googlers and from other  customers through our administrative  controls  and tenant isolation we've seen how  you can easily create keys in regions  and multi-regions and the consequence of  all these design decisions  is that it is as easy to protect your  data both  in google services and directly  as it is with a software key we'll have  all of these details  and more in an upcoming cloudhsn white  paper  so please keep your eye out for that  thank you
 

